;; A yield point in a module-level value.
;;
;; Every other way of being in the wrong place is some construct *inside* a
;; function. This one is outside all of them: a module-level `def` is a static
;; field, and its initializer runs in the class's static constructor. There is
;; no fiber under a static constructor to suspend and nothing to resume it, so
;; the question is not whether the member is async — it is that there is no
;; member of the user's making at all.
;;
;; Which is why the fix is different too. Everywhere else the answer is to
;; change the enclosing construct; here there is nothing to change, and the
;; value has to be computed somewhere a fiber is running instead.

;; EXPECT-ERROR: calling 'fetch' is a yield point
;; EXPECT-ERROR: yield point is not allowed here
;; EXPECT-ERROR: not inside a function at all
;; EXPECT-ERROR: which has no fiber under it to suspend

(: fetch (-> int int))
(defbjo (fetch x) (* x 2))

(: top int)
(def top (fetch 1))

(defun (main args) top)
